  "question": {
    "math_academy_id": "q-133965",
    "knowledge_point_id": "877509f5-a5de-55d2-ab5d-6c37fc5b0650",
    "knowledge_point": "Making a Prediction by Estimating a Trend Line",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/e3319694b1112e92602219492d51116d514a88e885431ac1ef0c8f8becb0dc18.png)\n\nA tea shop wants to determine the relationship between the outside temperature and the number of customers they serve. The scatter plot above shows the information they collected over several days. Assuming this trend continues, estimate the number of customers the tea shop expects to serve if the outside temperature is ${36}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}.$\n\n*Hint: Assume the trend is linear and that the trend line passes through the points $(15,60)$ and $(30,30).$*",
    "worked_solution": "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/7adb5dd9bfcfcea8842eebfcacc704237f010f87c8ffd695afab890801237abd.png)\n\nThe trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(15,60)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(30,30).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{30-60}{30-15} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-30)}{15} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-2$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-2x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(15,60):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x+b \\\\ 60 & =-2(15)+b \\\\ 60 & =-30+b \\\\ b & =90\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-2x+90.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=36$ into the equation above, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x+90 \\\\  & =-2(36)+90 \\\\  & =-72+90 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, the tea shop expects to serve $18$ customers.\n\nNote: The point where $x=36$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable.",
